this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday September 7th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  earlier this year China's cyberspace  regulator set out rules compelling  companies to share information about  their algorithms the systems that power  all the big online platforms  the goal of gathering this information  is to stop the negative effects of  algorithms from spreading misinformation  and infringing on privacy to online  harassment but experts warn that trying  to directly regulate algorithms could be  tricky since many of them adapt in  unpredictable ways based on user  Behavior  joining us to discuss how China is  taking on this Challenge and what other  countries might take away from their  efforts is our China Tech reporter Karen  Howe hi Karen thanks for being here hi  Zoe thanks so much for having me so with  Chinese regulation went into effect in  March in an effort to tighten controls  of algorithms underpinning the internet  then what happened the latest move is  they announced that they successfully  got submissions detailed documentation  from some of the most influential  internet companies in the country so  that's companies like Baidu Alibaba  tencent which makes the sort of  all-encompassing WeChat app and there  are about 30 algorithms from two dozen  companies that are supposed to be sort  of the core of the recommendation  systems of these platforms that have now  been filed to the top internet regulator  to describe exactly what they're doing  so what exactly has been made public and  what are Regulators getting from this  yeah so this is sort of like the first  step to this broader regulatory campaign  that China's trying to pursue where they  sort of almost very logically like if  they want to gain control over  algorithms they first want to gain  visibility so they're trying to get as  much information from companies to just  wrap their heads around what exactly  these systems even do but thus far  companies haven't actually submitted  code they haven't actually submitted  like highly technical information it's  actually more of a lay person's summary  of the operations of these algorithms so  what are the inputs what is the type of  user data that these companies are  collecting ultimately what is it trying  to do is it meant to personalize like a  social media app or is it meant to rank  search information in a search engine so  it's actually relatively high level  information but there's sort of a lot of  vagueness and uncertainty right now  around how much much more information  the government is going to start asking  these companies eventually  what do we know about beijing's kind of  thinking behind this and why it's  specifically gone after algorithms so  people familiar with the government's  thinking actually told us that Beijing  in general hadn't really been attuned to  this issue so in the US and EU there's  been like pretty long-standing debates  about the idea of how are we supposed to  regulate Tech platforms how are we  supposed to keep the internet safe and  algorithms have been a really big part  of this debate and this discussion but  for Beijing apparently the focus on  algorithms didn't really kick off until  Tick Tock was almost sold to an American  buyer so Tick Tock is obviously the  wildly popular social media app but it's  owned by a Chinese company called bite  dance and when it was going up for sale  it was right around the time when  Chinese Regulators also passed an export  control ban on information  recommendation technology and so it  suddenly became unclear whether Tick  Tock would be sold  with its algorithm and that suddenly  made bitters not as interested in buying  Tick Tock and this basically tipped off  Beijing to thinking oh wow algorithms  seem to be really important we should be  looking into this more and from there  they sort of realized how it could also  be extremely influential for public  opinion for social mobilization and that  was what kicked off these set of  regulatory actions to really get a  handle on these systems you mentioned  some other countries that have kind of  thought about this in the past have any  other nations taken these kinds of  regulatory steps to rein in algorithms  so in the U.S the short answer is no and  in the EU what's interesting is there's  been a pair of Acts that just recently  passed in July the Digital Services Act  and the digital markets act that try to  tackle the same question but actually go  about it in a more roundabout way so  with the digital Services act what the  EU tried to do was say okay we're not  not going to regulate algorithms  directly we're just going to tell Tech  platforms that they need to do  assessments of their entire products to  understand what the risks are and then  they have to decide how to mitigate  those risks and that could involve  tweaking the algorithm so it's a much  more indirect approach but the European  Union also thinks it's a more rights  preserving approach it's a more  democratic approach but critics also say  that it's sort of sidesteps some of the  issues and doesn't have as much teeth as  China's approach to actually trying to  literally get at the algorithms  themselves I mean is it even possible to  regulate these algorithms you know so  many of them are not concrete they  change they learn from users habits so  how do you actually rein them in as a  regulator  this is like the biggest question that I  think trying to sort of opened up no  one's really tried this before and so no  one really knows if it's going to work  but from like the basic principles of  this technology it would be incredibly  hard if not maybe impossible so internet  algorithms today are mostly based on  machine learning and machine learning is  when you have algorithms that aren't  actually written out explicitly as a set  of rules but they're actually just  learning from user data user Behavior to  figure out the rules themselves but most  machine learning systems are black boxes  like the people who coded them the  people who developed them can't actually  print out exactly what rules these  algorithms learned from the data so from  that perspective just like seeing the  code on these algorithms doesn't  actually get you very far in  understanding their logic and what  complicates it further is social media  platforms like Facebook or Tick Tock  they don't actually just use one  algorithm they use hundreds or thousands  of these interlocking algorithms and  they as you said are constantly being  updated based on a very complex stream  of user generated content user Behavior  so trying to fully understand every  dimension of what's going on is pretty  much impossible and therefore  controlling it is very very improbable  okay so what does this mean for Chinese  internet users you know post this  regulation coming into play  you know what's interesting is in the  Chinese context it might not actually  mean much different than what we've  already seen happening when cgp came  into Power there's been like a slow  tightening of a tightening and narrowing  of what discourse is actually allowed in  the public sphere and on these internet  platforms and from that perspective like  even if China ultimately can't figure  out exactly how to get these platforms  or this discourse to do exactly what it  wants to companies will ultimately still  take it as a signal that they need to  continue this trend of tightening down  narrowing down the discourse so in that  sense maybe it doesn't necessarily  matter in the Chinese context what I  think is particularly interesting about  this effort is more of what the rest of  the world might be able to learn learn  from its successes and failures because  it is the only attempt globally to  address this issue so directly through  the algorithmic level all right that's  our reporter Karen Howe Karen thanks so  much for joining us thank you so much  Zoe  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]
 

